SUMMARY:Saving the Songs
DESCRIPTION:With over 1\,200 species\, India has long been a land alive with birdsong. This discussion will explore why our birds are slipping away\, lost to vanishing habitats\, pesticide use\, hunting\, and to the hard lines of power cables and dams that redraw the land. What might it take to keep them here? \nSome absences\, like that of the Great Indian Bustard and White-bellied Heron\, are stark. But others are quieter\, easier to miss. Birds once “common\,” like drongos and rollers\, no longer gather as they did. Nightjars call less often from the forest’s edge. And the dawn chorus\, once a bright\, swelling thread stitching night to morning\, softens\, frays\, and fades. \nWhere have these birds gone? What does their silence mean for us? And can they be saved? \nIn collaboration with : \n   \nVenue: Auditorium\n\n\nIndia’s Vanishing Birdscapes and How We Can Save Them\nRSVP here (https://docs.google.com/forms/d/e/1FAIpQLSfJwyJSTY5-WEpngbJ8hZemRdg6IJF7MzDkY6o0LML-fCxdPA/viewform?usp=publish-editor)\n*Speakers*: Asad R Rahmani\, Suhel Quader\, Shweta Taneja\n*Event Categories*: Environment\, Wildlife
URL:https://bangaloreinternationalcentre.org/event/saving-the-songs/
LOCATION:Bangalore International Centre\, 7\, 4th Main Road\, Domlur II Stage\, Bangalore\, Karnataka\, 560071\, India
CATEGORIES:Environment,Wildlife
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://bangaloreinternationalcentre.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/Thumbnail_STS.jpg
END:VEVENT
