SUMMARY:After the Wave
DESCRIPTION:Climate change is already here\, woven into the mundane and the everyday. This conversation on the novel Under Water explores what literary fiction can capture about the natural world that dystopian “cli-fi” often cannot. It touches on the limits and possibilities of fiction when it comes to representing the environment\, climate change\, and grief. \nThe novel moves between two large-scale disasters\, but it lingers longest on something smaller: the slow\, gradual\, often invisible changes to local ecosystems\, what critic Rob Nixon called “slow violence.” In The Great Derangement\, Amitav Ghosh argued that serious literary fiction has largely failed to represent climate change at all\, leaving the subject to genre and spectacle. Under Water is offered as an answer to that gap\, fiction that holds both the freak event and the quiet\, cumulative truth of a changing world. \nIn collaboration with: \n \n\n\nWhat Literary Fiction Can Teach Us About Climate Change\nRSVP here (https://docs.google.com/forms/d/e/1FAIpQLSdF7ViQDAV9bV8MeVObrTeZ6rYod86wigRSZxcy7J4a465kJw/viewform?usp=publish-editor)\n*Speakers*: Tara Menon\, Keshava Guha\n*Event Categories*: Books\, Climate Change\, Environment\, Fiction
